WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.392
A file upload introduces untrusted content
and untrusted

2
00:00:03.392 --> 00:00:06.104
metadata into several parts of an
application.

3
00:00:06.104 --> 00:00:09.177
Receiving the HTTP request is only the
beginning.

4
00:00:09.177 --> 00:00:11.889
Multipart parsing separates fields and
file streams.

5
00:00:11.889 --> 00:00:14.962
Temporary storage or memory buffering
holds the bytes.

6
00:00:14.962 --> 00:00:17.673
Validation classifies them, persistence
assigns ownership, and

7
00:00:17.673 --> 00:00:20.566
processors may decode, transform, or scan
them.

8
00:00:20.566 --> 00:00:22.825
Later, a download endpoint or public

9
00:00:22.825 --> 00:00:25.537
delivery service interprets the stored
record again.

10
00:00:25.537 --> 00:00:28.249
Each transition needs an explicit trust
decision.

11
00:00:28.249 --> 00:00:31.412
The original filename, extension, declared
media type, and

12
00:00:31.412 --> 00:00:34.124
claimed size all come from the sender.

13
00:00:34.124 --> 00:00:37.197
So do the file contents, embedded
metadata, archive

14
00:00:37.197 --> 00:00:40.722
entry names, and any submitted tenant or
destination identifiers.

15
00:00:40.722 --> 00:00:44.970
Authentication identifies a caller; it
does not make those values reliable.

16
00:00:44.970 --> 00:00:46.778
TypeScript interfaces and assertions also

17
00:00:46.778 --> 00:00:48.766
provide no runtime file validation.

18
00:00:48.766 --> 00:00:51.297
Renaming a request object to a trusted

19
00:00:51.297 --> 00:00:53.828
upload type changes the compiler's
assumptions without

20
00:00:53.828 --> 00:00:56.540
changing the evidence available to the
service.

21
00:00:56.540 --> 00:01:00.155
Writing a file buffer to a path formed
from an

22
00:01:00.155 --> 00:01:03.951
upload directory and the original filename
hides several independent assumptions.

23
00:01:03.951 --> 00:01:06.934
It assumes the filename chooses an
acceptable destination,

24
00:01:06.934 --> 00:01:09.465
the resulting path stays inside the
intended

25
00:01:09.465 --> 00:01:12.628
root, and an existing object may be
overwritten.

26
00:01:12.628 --> 00:01:16.696
It also assumes the full buffer fits
safely in memory, the

27
00:01:16.696 --> 00:01:20.944
format is permitted, and future consumers
can handle the stored bytes.

28
00:01:20.944 --> 00:01:24.650
A path joining function does not establish
those guarantees, and

29
00:01:24.650 --> 00:01:28.446
a successful write establishes none of the
missing file checks.

30
00:01:28.446 --> 00:01:31.519
Design the pipeline so those decisions
remain visible.

31
00:01:31.519 --> 00:01:34.592
Authorize the intended operation,
constrain incoming work, validate

32
00:01:34.592 --> 00:01:37.575
the supported file class, assign storage
identity on

33
00:01:37.575 --> 00:01:40.739
the server, and isolate content from
application code.

34
00:01:40.739 --> 00:01:43.812
Then control every later use of the bytes.

35
00:01:43.812 --> 00:01:46.071
Temporary files need the same path,

36
00:01:46.071 --> 00:01:48.873
permission, and cleanup discipline as
permanent objects.

37
00:01:48.873 --> 00:01:51.766
Public availability should be a deliberate
state reached

38
00:01:51.766 --> 00:01:54.387
after required checks, rather than an
accidental

39
00:01:54.387 --> 00:01:57.460
consequence of where an upload happened to
land.

